# Python review checklist

Ported from the deadchannel project's `docs/agents/python-review-checklist.md`, which is itself a port of the author's shared checklist.
The ledger (§1–§12) is carried over verbatim; the repo adaptations are rewritten for LocalRAG.
It complements the other rules in `.cursor/rules/` rather than replacing them: `critical-rules.mdc`, `python-conventions.mdc` and `testing.mdc` are the baseline conventions, and this file is the deeper, itemized pass.

Walk it before merging any new Python code in `localrag/`, `evals/` or `tests/`.

## How to use this file (read before reviewing)

1. **Copy, don't regenerate.** Copy this file to a working path for the diff
   under review — e.g. `.review/<branch-or-pr>-checklist.md`. Never rebuild
   the list from memory: the completeness guarantee lives in *this file*,
   not in attention on any given run.
2. **Fill every row.** For each rule set `Verdict` to `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `N/A`:
   - `PASS` — the rule applies to this diff and the code satisfies it. Cite
     the strongest `file:line` you checked in `Evidence`.
   - `FAIL` — the rule applies and the code violates it. Cite `file:line`
     and state the fix in `Note`.
   - `N/A` — the rule cannot apply to this diff (no code of that kind
     changed) **or** it is a `[RUFF]` rule already covered by a green Ruff
     gate. Say which in `Note`. `N/A` is a conscious decision, never a
     silent skip.
3. **Completion gate — do not produce findings until zero `Verdict` cells
   are blank.** A blank cell means the rule was not considered.
4. **Derive the findings from the ledger, not from memory.** The review
   summary is built by filtering the `FAIL` rows into `[CRITICAL]` then
   `[STANDARD]`. If a rule is not in the ledger as `FAIL`, it is not a
   finding.

`[RUFF]` rows: LocalRAG's Ruff gate runs `select = ["ALL"]`, so mark a `[RUFF]` rule `N/A` with note `ruff-gate-green` when the gate is green and the rule is not in the `pyproject.toml` ignore list.
Only flag a `[RUFF]` rule `FAIL` when the gate does not cover it.

IDs are stable (`R<section>.<n>`). If a rule here changes, keep the ID.

## Repo adaptations

Standing exceptions for this repository.
Apply them without re-litigating, and add to this list (with evidence) when a review surfaces another one.

- **R1.2 / R1.10 / R1.11 (canonical module names, three-layer layout)**: LocalRAG's layout is the one recorded in `AGENTS.md` ("Package map" and "Basic DDD layout"): the HTTP entry is `localrag/api/main.py`, config is `localrag/settings.py`, use cases live in `localrag/application/`, and domain packages (`ingestion/`, `rag/`, `storage/`, …) are organized by RAG concern rather than as `controller/` / `service/` / `repository/`.
  New code follows that layout; the rules still bind *within* it (thin adapters, no `helpers.py` / `utils.py`, no leading-underscore module names).
- **R5.9 (`Protocol` only as a runtime seam)**: `localrag/plugins/retriever.py`'s `RetrieverPlugin` / `RetrieverContract` pass — third-party retriever plugins are the real runtime implementations (ADR-recorded plugin contract).
- **R7.3 (custom exceptions in a dedicated module)**: application-level errors live in `localrag/application/errors.py`; HTTP-mapped ones are `HttpMappedError` subclasses in `localrag/api/exceptions.py`.
- **R5.7 / R9.4 (no work-item numbers in code)**: doesn't bind an ADR cited as the settled design record for a decision.
  It does bind GitHub issue numbers, "see the owning PR", and point-in-time status words ("yet", "today", "currently", "not yet built") — encode issue numbers in the commit message or the ADR, as `change-discipline.mdc` already requires.

## §1 Module structure & architecture [REVIEW]

| ID | Sev | Rule | Verdict | Evidence (file:line) | Note |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| R1.1 | STANDARD | DDD layering: controller / service / repository separated; endpoints delegate to a service, no inline business logic | | | |
| R1.2 | STANDARD | The transport entry module (`api.py`) stays thin - bootstrap / wiring / lifespan only | | | |
| R1.3 | STANDARD | Large literals extracted to a dedicated module (`system_prompt.py` for the system prompt), not inlined | | | |
| R1.4 | STANDARD | Reusable helpers extracted into their own modules, not scattered | | | |
| R1.5 | STANDARD | One class / cohesive unit per module; over-large packages split into subpackages; a re-export facade `__init__.py` ONLY at a distribution boundary (i.e: a lib) — inside a service, empty `__init__.py` + absolute module imports | | | |
| R1.6 | STANDARD | Dependencies point inward: service never touches transport types; controller never skips service to reach repository | | | |
| R1.7 | STANDARD | SOLID; composition over inheritance; single responsibility per unit | | | |
| R1.8 | STANDARD | YAGNI bounds abstraction - no generalization until reused/non-trivial; trivial single-use logic stays inline | | | |
| R1.9 | STANDARD | Behavior lives with the state it owns: self-contained state transitions / invariants / calculations go on the domain model (anemic model is an anti-pattern); logic moves to a service only when it spans aggregates or needs an injected collaborator (repo/HTTP/AWS) | | | |
| R1.10 | STANDARD | Canonical module names — `api.py` (transport entry, never `main.py`), `settings.py` (config, never `config.py`), `system_prompt.py` (never `prompts.py`); no leading-underscore module names inside a package | | | |
| R1.11 | STANDARD | Three-layer package layout named after the layers: `controller/` (every exposed surface - HTTP endpoints and LLM-facing `@tool` in `controller/tools/`; declares and delegates only), `service/` (logic in by-capability subpackages, never `helpers.py`/`utils.py`), `repository/` (every outbound boundary - persistence, object stores, SDK/HTTP peers; no separate `gateway/`/`infrastructure/`). Bounded by R1.8 - no ceremonial empty layers | | | |
| R1.12 | STANDARD | No pass-through wrapper — a method whose whole body forwards to a collaborator's identically-named method is deleted and the caller calls the owner; validating/adapting/narrowing wrappers are fine | | | |

## §2 Configuration & typing [REVIEW]

| ID | Sev | Rule | Verdict | Evidence (file:line) | Note |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| R2.1 | STANDARD | Pydantic settings for env config (not `os.environ.get`); import `BaseSettings` from `pydantic_settings`, never from `pydantic` | | | |
| R2.2 | STANDARD | Config values (secret IDs, resource names, toggles) live in the settings module, not scattered through business code | | | |
| R2.3 | **CRITICAL** | Strong typing over runtime casts — no `cast(...)`; validate boundaries with a typed model | | | |
| R2.4 | STANDARD | Typed structure (model/dataclass/`TypedDict`) whenever the key set is known & fixed; `dict[str, X]` only for open/dynamic keys with uniform `X` | | | |
| R2.5 | STANDARD | No stringly-typed returns — structured results return a typed object/enum, not a bare `str` the caller must parse | | | |

