## §7 Error handling [RUFF]

| ID | Sev | Rule | Verdict | Evidence (file:line) | Note |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| R7.1 | **CRITICAL** | Specific exceptions; never catch bare `Exception` (`BLE001`) | | | |
| R7.2 | STANDARD | Propagate, don't swallow - `raise NewError(...) from err` | | | |
| R7.3 | STANDARD | Custom exceptions in a dedicated module (RFC 9457 types where the code is an HTTP service) — see "Repo adaptations" above for where they live here; never declared inline at the raise site | | | |

## §8 Type annotations [mixed]

| ID | Sev | Rule | Verdict | Evidence (file:line) | Note |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| R8.1 | **CRITICAL** | `\|` union syntax; never `Optional` / `Union` (`UP007` / `UP045`) | | | |
| R8.2 | STANDARD | Plain defaults set directly; no `Field(default=...)` *just* to set a default. `Field(...)` still required for metadata/constraints; mutable defaults use `Field(default_factory=...)` | | | |

## §9 Docstrings & comments [mixed]

| ID | Sev | Rule | Verdict | Evidence (file:line) | Note |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| R9.1 | STANDARD | No forced docstrings on auto-explicative code; write one only when it adds what code can't say; when present it follows **Google style**. The gate enforces format (`D2xx`/`D4xx`), never presence - `D1xx` (`D100`-`D107`) must be in the repo's `ignore`; a repo where `D1xx` is active is misconfigured | | | |
| R9.2 | STANDARD | Comments explain the *why* (a decision/tradeoff), never the *what*; no AI-filler narration | | | |
| R9.3 | **CRITICAL** | `__init__.py` is empty **or** a pure re-export facade - never logic/side-effects; re-exports linter-clean (`__all__` / redundant alias, no `F401`) and eager-import-cheap. `__all__` appears ONLY in such a facade, never in a regular module | | | |
| R9.4 | **CRITICAL** | Comments/docstrings survive loss of context - no gap/spike/plan ids, "see the owning PR", work-item numbers, sprint/phase labels, or point-in-time status ("as of this writing", "currently unresolved", "decided", "not yet built"). See "Repo adaptations" above for the ADR/issue-citation exception | | | |
| R9.5 | STANDARD | Plain prose, Google style, no Sphinx roles (`:class:`/`:mod:`/`:meth:`/`:func:`/`:attr:`/`:data:`/`:exc:`) - nothing renders reST; `Args:`/`Returns:`/`Raises:` proper Google style and only where the signature can't say it | | | |
| R9.6 | STANDARD | No section-banner comments (`# ---- Public surface ----`, decorative rules in a settings class) - ordering/naming carry it; a banner usually means the module does too much | | | |
| R9.7 | STANDARD | Every stated rationale checked against the artifact it claims (`pyproject.toml` for a dependency claim, the signature, the settings module) — a false reason (e.g. `# noqa: PLC0415 - optional heavy dependency` on an unconditional dep) is worse than none | | | |
| R9.8 | RUFF | No `# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-` source-encoding declaration (UTF-8 is the default since Python 3.0) - `UP009`; check the gate actually selects it | | | |

## §10 Testing [REVIEW]

| ID | Sev | Rule | Verdict | Evidence (file:line) | Note |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| R10.1 | **CRITICAL** | `@pytest.mark.parametrize` for variations, not near-duplicate `test_with_500` / `test_with_502` functions | | | |
| R10.2 | **CRITICAL** | No isolated `__init__` or model tests - no `test_init_*`, no test files for Pydantic models/dataclasses | | | |
| R10.3 | STANDARD | Whole-object assertions over field-picking (field-level only when one field is the behavior under test) | | | |
| R10.4 | STANDARD | Mock at the boundary (external services/DB/HTTP/AWS/I/O); real objects for internal structures; stub over mock when behavior is simple | | | |
| R10.5 | STANDARD | No AAA comments (`# Arrange/# Act/# Assert`) - separate sections with blank lines | | | |
| R10.6 | STANDARD | Deterministic tests - `freezegun` for dates; no outbound HTTP (autouse conftest blocks network; `@pytest.mark.no_requests` only when needed) | | | |
| R10.7 | STANDARD | Test layout mirrors source (`app/controller/tools/search.py` -> `tests/controller/tools/test_search.py`) | | | |
| R10.8 | STANDARD | Reusable fixtures in `tests/fixtures/` scoped by reach; general ones registered in `conftest.py`; a once-used fixture stays local | | | |

## §11 Concurrency & race conditions [REVIEW]

| ID | Sev | Rule | Verdict | Evidence (file:line) | Note |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| R11.1 | **CRITICAL** | Any change touching shared state (requests/APIs, DB transactions, caches, queues, files, counters) is explicitly reasoned about for race conditions | | | |
| R11.2 | **CRITICAL** | In-process async races checked: no check-then-act split across an `await`; request-scoped state kept local or guarded | | | |
| R11.3 | **CRITICAL** | Every other reader/writer of the same state identified and the interaction confirmed | | | |
| R11.4 | **CRITICAL** | Atomic ops / transaction isolation / idempotency / optimistic concurrency preferred over read-modify-write & check-then-act; concurrency boundary named, not hand-waved | | | |

## §12 Resource lifecycle: caches, connections & buffering [REVIEW]

| ID | Sev | Rule | Verdict | Evidence (file:line) | Note |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| R12.1 | **CRITICAL** | Any cache/registry keyed by request/session/conversation id is bounded (LRU/TTL) and evicted on a terminal lifecycle event - applies to module-global *and* DI-held dicts | | | |
| R12.2 | **CRITICAL** | Pooled boundary clients (`httpx`, DB pool) built once and reused via `lifespan`/`app.state`; a scoped client is `with`/`async with`-scoped or explicitly `close()`'d | | | |
| R12.3 | STANDARD | No full materialization of a stream/file/response when incremental handling is possible; "relay"/pass-through code flushes in bounded chunks; held payloads capped with a truncation marker | | | |
| R12.4 | STANDARD | Every awaited external I/O has explicit timeouts on both legs (connect and read/idle); no `read=None` | | | |
| R12.5 | STANDARD | Background/detached tasks holding large payloads are concurrency-bounded (semaphore or total-bytes cap) | | | |

